.TH "server_socket_close" "3" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server library"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqserver.h\(dq

void server_socket_close(int fd, const char *path);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Closes the listening socket file descriptor and removes the associated
socket file from the filesystem.
.PP
If \f[CR]fd\f[R] is non\-negative, calls \f[CR]close(fd)\f[R].
If \f[CR]path\f[R] is non\-NULL, calls \f[CR]unlink(path)\f[R] to remove
the socket file.
Either parameter may safely be \f[CR]\-1\f[R] or \f[CR]NULL\f[R]
respectively.
.PP
This function is typically called during server shutdown to release the
listening socket and clean up the filesystem entry.
.SH PARAMETERS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]fd\f[R] \(en The listening socket file descriptor to close.
If negative, no close is performed.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]path\f[R] \(en Filesystem path to the Unix domain socket file.
If NULL, no unlink is performed.
.SH RETURN VALUE
None.
.SH ERRORS
Errors from \f[CR]close()\f[R] and \f[CR]unlink()\f[R] are silently
ignored.
This is intentional for shutdown paths where the socket may already be
closed or the file may already be removed.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[CR]server_socket_create\f[R](3), \f[CR]server_shutdown\f[R](3),
\f[CR]close\f[R](2), \f[CR]unlink\f[R](2)
.SH NOTES
Called by \f[CR]server_shutdown()\f[R] as part of the clean shutdown
sequence.
Also called on error paths during server startup if socket creation
succeeds but subsequent initialization (e.g., token generation) fails.
.PP
The function does not remove the parent directory.
Directory cleanup is left to the operating system or the user.
